---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import JsonLd from '../components/JsonLd.astro';
import FeatureList from '../components/FeatureList.astro';
import { IOT_FEATURES, STATUS, product } from '../lib/products';

const p = product('iot');
---

<Base
  title="HotLoop IoT: Home Assistant, rewritten in Go for OT"
  description="HotLoop IoT is the Home Assistant feature set rewritten natively in Go for the plant floor. It arrives in an upcoming release, once MQTT discovery lands."
  ogAlt="HotLoop IoT, Home Assistant rewritten in Go for OT."
>
  <JsonLd
    slot="head"
    data={{
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'SoftwareApplication',
      name: p.name,
      url: 'https://hotloop.io/iot/',
      description:
        'The Home Assistant feature set rewritten natively in Go for operational technology: entities, automations, helpers, alarms, notifications, MCP, and MQTT with discovery, plus industrial protocol drivers.',
      applicationCategory: 'BusinessApplication',
      operatingSystem: 'Linux',
      license: 'https://hotloop.io/license/',
      isAccessibleForFree: true,
      publisher: { '@id': 'https://hotloop.io/#organization' },
    }}
  />
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap stack">
      <div class="cluster"><span class="label">HotLoop IoT</span><span class="chip warn">Upcoming release</span></div>
      <h1>Home Assistant, rewritten in Go for the plant floor.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Everything Home Assistant does that a factory actually wants, rebuilt natively: entities, automations,
        helpers, scripts, the logbook, dashboards, notifications and MCP, on MQTT with discovery, with every
        industrial protocol driver HotLoop has. One static binary. No Python, no add-on store, no privileged
        container sniffing the LAN for light bulbs.
      </p>
      <div class="notice info">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9" /><path d="M12 8h.01M11 12h1v5h1" /></svg>
        <div>
          <strong>HotLoop IoT is not out yet. It arrives in an upcoming release.</strong>
          <p>
            It ships once MQTT discovery lands, so the first version finds your Shelly, ESPHome, Tasmota and
            Zigbee2MQTT devices by itself instead of making you type in topics. There is no image or chart to install
            today, and no date, because a date we miss is worse than none. Most of what it will carry already runs
            inside the <a href="/gateway/">Gateway</a>, which you can install now.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cluster">
        <a class="btn primary" href="/gateway/#install">Install the Gateway today</a>
        <a class="btn" href="/products/">Compare the lineup</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap grid grid-2 split">
      <div class="stack">
        <span class="label">Why it exists</span>
        <h2>We do not run Home Assistant. We rebuilt what it does.</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          Home Assistant got the model right: everything is an entity, rules react to entities, and the people in
          the building own a few values of their own. It was also built for somebody's living room, in Python, with
          thousands of integrations for gadgets no plant has ever owned. Pointing that at a PLC is how you end
          up with a light switch UI on a press line.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          So IoT is the model, rebuilt in Go, for OT. We don't embed Home Assistant, bridge to it, or fork it. Every
          command still goes through the one write gate, and a failed read is bad quality, never a zero.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        <span class="label">The base of the lineup</span>
        <h2>Everything above IoT is IoT plus more.</h2>
        <p class="muted">
          <a href="/edge/">Edge</a> is IoT plus the machine layer. The <a href="/gateway/">Gateway</a> is everything
          plus fleet, multi-site and reports. The <a href="/edge-relay/">Edge Relay</a> is the headless one. One
          codebase, one tag, four images, released together.
        </p>
        <p class="muted">
          Its database is <strong>{p.databases}</strong>, picked by a setting, in the same image. SQLite for a Pi
          on a shelf, PostgreSQL when it grows up, and moving up to Edge or the Gateway is designed to keep your data
          and your config.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="label">What is in it</span>
        <h2>Each piece says where it actually stands.</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          "{STATUS.released.label}" means it ships in the published Gateway today. "{STATUS.main.label}" means merged
          and landing in the next release. "{STATUS.building.label}" means somebody is writing it right now.
          "{STATUS.planned.label}" means exactly that.
        </p>
      </div>
      <FeatureList features={IOT_FEATURES} />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="card cta reveal">
        <div class="stack">
          <span class="label">Licensing</span>
          <h2>Free, with no license key, when it ships.</h2>
          <p class="muted">
            IoT is under the HotLoop Community License, like the Gateway, Edge and the Edge Relay. Individuals use it
            free. Businesses sign up for <a href="https://embernet.ai">EmberNET</a> free and use it there free. There
            are no tiers to unlock and no key to type in.
          </p>
        </div>
        <a class="btn primary" href="/license/">Read the terms</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .page-hero { padding-block: clamp(var(--hl-s7), 7vw, var(--hl-s9)) var(--hl-s7); }
  .page-hero h1 { max-width: 20ch; }
  .page-hero .notice { max-width: 68ch; }
  .split { align-items: start; gap: var(--hl-s8); }
  .cta { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: var(--hl-s6); justify-content: space-between; align-items: center; padding: clamp(var(--hl-s6), 5vw, var(--hl-s8)); }
  .cta .stack { max-width: 62ch; }
</style>
